1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,040
Here are two identical
sealed tubes of hydrogen

2
00:00:03,120 --> 00:00:06,000
and purple iodine vapour,
heated to the same

3
00:00:06,080 --> 00:00:06,640
temperature.

4
00:00:07,280 --> 00:00:09,120
We add a pinch of a metal catalyst

5
00:00:09,160 --> 00:00:10,400
to the tube on the right.

6
00:00:10,960 --> 00:00:13,160
A catalyst is a substance that speeds a

7
00:00:13,200 --> 00:00:15,920
reaction up without being used up.

8
00:00:16,240 --> 00:00:17,120
Watch the purple.

9
00:00:17,600 --> 00:00:20,160
In the catalyst tube it fades fast, in

10
00:00:20,240 --> 00:00:21,520
the other slowly.

11
00:00:22,080 --> 00:00:24,080
But wait long enough, and both end up

12
00:00:24,160 --> 00:00:25,600
the same pale shade.

13
00:00:26,160 --> 00:00:28,880
So a catalyst changed how fast, but not

14
00:00:29,040 --> 00:00:30,160
where it ends.

15
00:00:30,400 --> 00:00:32,080
How can it change one and not the

16
00:00:32,241 --> 00:00:32,640
other?

17
00:00:33,867 --> 00:00:36,186
Most collisions just bounce apart.

18
00:00:36,427 --> 00:00:39,226
Only collisions with enough
energy make a reaction

19
00:00:39,307 --> 00:00:39,906
happen.

20
00:00:40,187 --> 00:00:42,666
The minimum energy
a collision needs is called

21
00:00:42,707 --> 00:00:44,386
the activation energy.

22
00:00:44,667 --> 00:00:47,266
Picture it as a ridge between two valleys.

23
00:00:47,467 --> 00:00:50,186
The height is energy: each valley is the

24
00:00:50,267 --> 00:00:52,026
energy stored in those molecules.

25
00:00:52,587 --> 00:00:55,706
Hydrogen and iodine sit
in one valley, hydrogen

26
00:00:55,867 --> 00:00:58,026
iodide in the other, and the top of

27
00:00:58,067 --> 00:01:00,106
the ridge is the energy a collision must

28
00:01:00,187 --> 00:01:00,586
reach.

29
00:01:01,147 --> 00:01:03,706
Hydrogen iodide has
to climb the same ridge

30
00:01:03,747 --> 00:01:04,586
to go back.

31
00:01:05,800 --> 00:01:09,240
A catalyst gives the molecules
an alternative pathway,

32
00:01:09,320 --> 00:01:10,440
a different route.

33
00:01:10,680 --> 00:01:13,000
They land on the metal, split into atoms

34
00:01:13,080 --> 00:01:15,560
that stick to the surface, the atoms pair

35
00:01:15,640 --> 00:01:18,440
up as hydrogen iodide and leave.

36
00:01:18,520 --> 00:01:21,000
The metal ends up unchanged, ready for the

37
00:01:21,080 --> 00:01:22,120
next molecules.

38
00:01:23,334 --> 00:01:25,573
Even the hardest step on that route needs

39
00:01:25,734 --> 00:01:28,133
less energy than the old ridge, so the

40
00:01:28,214 --> 00:01:29,493
new pass is lower.

41
00:01:30,054 --> 00:01:32,293
The valleys have not moved, so the energy

42
00:01:32,374 --> 00:01:34,693
difference between them
is the same as before.

43
00:01:35,334 --> 00:01:37,973
That difference is the
heat of the reaction:

44
00:01:38,094 --> 00:01:40,613
going down the slope gives out heat, going

45
00:01:40,694 --> 00:01:41,973
up takes it in.

46
00:01:43,200 --> 00:01:46,000
A lower pass means
a faster reaction, because

47
00:01:46,080 --> 00:01:48,640
more collisions have enough
energy to get through.

48
00:01:49,200 --> 00:01:51,840
Of the same twenty
collisions, say one gets

49
00:01:51,920 --> 00:01:54,080
over the high ridge, and ten get through

50
00:01:54,120 --> 00:01:54,960
the low pass.

51
00:01:55,440 --> 00:01:58,160
That is ten times as many
successful collisions

52
00:01:58,320 --> 00:01:59,200
every second.

53
00:01:59,840 --> 00:02:02,720
Hydrogen iodide going
back climbs to the same

54
00:02:02,800 --> 00:02:03,520
new top.

55
00:02:03,920 --> 00:02:06,320
The valleys have not moved, so its climb

56
00:02:06,400 --> 00:02:08,000
is lower by the same height.

57
00:02:08,640 --> 00:02:10,920
A drop of the same height multiplies the

58
00:02:10,960 --> 00:02:14,080
successful collisions by
the same factor, whichever side

59
00:02:14,120 --> 00:02:17,360
you start from, so its
successful collisions also

60
00:02:17,440 --> 00:02:19,280
rise ten times.

61
00:02:19,440 --> 00:02:21,040
Ten is a made-up factor.

62
00:02:22,267 --> 00:02:23,626
Follow both tubes.

63
00:02:24,107 --> 00:02:26,586
The tube without
the catalyst starts with nine

64
00:02:26,667 --> 00:02:29,786
units of hydrogen, nine of iodine and no

65
00:02:29,867 --> 00:02:31,066
hydrogen iodide.

66
00:02:31,627 --> 00:02:34,746
The forward rate, which
makes hydrogen iodide, starts

67
00:02:34,827 --> 00:02:37,466
high, at about four units of hydrogen used

68
00:02:37,547 --> 00:02:40,266
per tick of our made-up clock, and falls.

69
00:02:40,827 --> 00:02:43,706
The reverse rate starts at zero and rises.

70
00:02:44,267 --> 00:02:46,346
At about thirteen on the clock the rates

71
00:02:46,427 --> 00:02:48,586
are nearly equal, close to a fifth of

72
00:02:48,640 --> 00:02:50,986
a unit per tick, and the mixture settles

73
00:02:51,067 --> 00:02:53,386
at about two units of hydrogen, two of

74
00:02:53,467 --> 00:02:56,426
iodine and fourteen of hydrogen iodide.

75
00:02:57,634 --> 00:02:59,633
Now the tube with the catalyst, with the

76
00:02:59,714 --> 00:03:00,513
same start.

77
00:03:00,994 --> 00:03:03,793
At any mixture, its rates are ten times

78
00:03:03,874 --> 00:03:06,273
the first tube's rates
at that same mixture.

79
00:03:06,914 --> 00:03:09,073
So every change takes a tenth of the

80
00:03:09,154 --> 00:03:12,113
time, thirteen divided
by ten, which is one

81
00:03:12,274 --> 00:03:12,913
point three.

82
00:03:13,554 --> 00:03:15,953
It follows the same path, like the same

83
00:03:16,034 --> 00:03:18,193
film played at ten times the speed.

84
00:03:19,400 --> 00:03:20,920
Why the same mixture?

85
00:03:21,160 --> 00:03:23,480
In the first tube, that mixture has rates

86
00:03:23,560 --> 00:03:25,360
of a fifth and a fifth.

87
00:03:25,640 --> 00:03:27,960
With the catalyst, ten times a fifth is

88
00:03:28,040 --> 00:03:30,600
two, and ten times a fifth is two.

89
00:03:30,840 --> 00:03:32,840
The rates are still equal there.

90
00:03:33,160 --> 00:03:35,960
Any other mixture has
unequal rates, and ten

91
00:03:36,040 --> 00:03:38,280
times unequal is still unequal.

92
00:03:38,840 --> 00:03:41,160
The tube stops changing
only where the rates

93
00:03:41,240 --> 00:03:43,560
are equal, and that is still the same

94
00:03:43,640 --> 00:03:44,280
mixture.

95
00:03:45,500 --> 00:03:47,100
Stop the clock early, though.

96
00:03:47,500 --> 00:03:49,980
At time three, the first tube has about

97
00:03:50,060 --> 00:03:53,020
ten units of hydrogen
iodide, and the catalyst

98
00:03:53,180 --> 00:03:55,340
tube already has fourteen.

99
00:03:55,460 --> 00:03:58,060
So if the clock stops early, a catalyst

100
00:03:58,140 --> 00:03:59,820
gives more product.

101
00:03:59,940 --> 00:04:02,220
After a long time, both tubes hold the

102
00:04:02,340 --> 00:04:03,100
same amount.

103
00:04:04,300 --> 00:04:05,980
What if we add the catalyst to a

104
00:04:06,180 --> 00:04:08,100
tube that has already settled?

105
00:04:08,380 --> 00:04:10,540
Both rates are a fifth and the amounts

106
00:04:10,620 --> 00:04:11,660
are steady.

107
00:04:11,740 --> 00:04:14,380
The catalyst makes both
rates ten times bigger

108
00:04:14,460 --> 00:04:15,980
at the same moment.

109
00:04:16,020 --> 00:04:18,540
They are two and two, still equal, so

110
00:04:18,580 --> 00:04:20,620
the amounts do not change.

111
00:04:20,660 --> 00:04:22,380
There is a moment of the change, but

112
00:04:22,460 --> 00:04:25,500
no response, because
neither rate pulls ahead.

113
00:04:26,700 --> 00:04:29,660
Adding a reacting
substance changed one rate.

114
00:04:29,900 --> 00:04:32,700
Heating, or squeezing
a gas with different gas

115
00:04:32,860 --> 00:04:35,420
counts on each side, changed both rates by

116
00:04:35,580 --> 00:04:38,780
different factors, so one
reaction pulled ahead and

117
00:04:38,860 --> 00:04:40,140
the mixture shifted.

118
00:04:40,460 --> 00:04:42,860
When we heated, the reaction that takes in

119
00:04:42,940 --> 00:04:45,420
heat gained most, and that is the one

120
00:04:45,500 --> 00:04:46,540
with the higher ridge.

121
00:04:47,100 --> 00:04:50,380
Squeezing hydrogen and iodine,
with two gas particles

122
00:04:50,420 --> 00:04:52,940
on each side, raised both rates by the

123
00:04:53,060 --> 00:04:55,220
same factor, and nothing shifted.

124
00:04:55,500 --> 00:04:57,980
A catalyst lowers both ridges by the same

125
00:04:58,060 --> 00:05:00,700
height, so it is like that case: both

126
00:05:00,780 --> 00:05:03,260
rates rise by the same factor and nothing

127
00:05:03,340 --> 00:05:04,140
pulls ahead.

128
00:05:05,367 --> 00:05:06,726
Here is a shortcut.

129
00:05:06,967 --> 00:05:10,486
A catalyst changes the speed,
not the destination.

130
00:05:10,887 --> 00:05:13,446
It tells you how soon the mixture arrives,

131
00:05:13,607 --> 00:05:15,286
never what the mixture is.

132
00:05:16,500 --> 00:05:17,460
Your turn.

133
00:05:17,780 --> 00:05:21,780
Nitrogen and hydrogen make ammonia, NH₃,

134
00:05:21,820 --> 00:05:23,380
in the Haber process.

135
00:05:23,700 --> 00:05:27,220
Two identical rigid sealed
vessels start with only

136
00:05:27,300 --> 00:05:30,420
nitrogen and hydrogen, at
the same steady temperature.

137
00:05:30,900 --> 00:05:33,620
Imagine both are left
long enough to settle;

138
00:05:33,780 --> 00:05:36,020
without a catalyst that
takes an extremely long

139
00:05:36,100 --> 00:05:36,500
time.

140
00:05:36,900 --> 00:05:40,340
Vessel B contains iron,
a catalyst, and vessel

141
00:05:40,500 --> 00:05:41,460
A does not.

142
00:05:42,100 --> 00:05:44,740
One: which vessel settles first?

143
00:05:44,980 --> 00:05:46,980
Which has more ammonia if we stop the

144
00:05:47,060 --> 00:05:50,180
clock early, before
either vessel has settled?

145
00:05:50,340 --> 00:05:53,100
And which has more once both have settled?

146
00:05:53,300 --> 00:05:55,740
Explain it using activation energy.

147
00:05:56,100 --> 00:05:58,900
Two: a third vessel has already settled at

148
00:05:58,980 --> 00:06:01,300
equilibrium and we add iron.

149
00:06:01,380 --> 00:06:03,620
What happens to the forward
rate, the reverse

150
00:06:03,700 --> 00:06:06,700
rate and the amount of ammonia, and why?

151
00:06:06,980 --> 00:06:08,980
Pause the video if you'd like more time.

152
00:07:24,400 --> 00:07:24,800
One.

153
00:07:25,040 --> 00:07:27,320
Vessel B settles first.

154
00:07:27,600 --> 00:07:29,520
Iron gives the molecules a route with a

155
00:07:29,600 --> 00:07:33,040
lower activation energy,
so more collisions succeed in

156
00:07:33,120 --> 00:07:34,360
both directions.

157
00:07:34,640 --> 00:07:37,520
If you stop early,
before either has settled,

158
00:07:37,600 --> 00:07:39,520
B has more ammonia.

159
00:07:39,640 --> 00:07:41,840
Once both have settled, they hold the same

160
00:07:42,040 --> 00:07:44,800
amount, however long vessel A takes.

161
00:07:44,880 --> 00:07:47,200
The iron lowers
the activation energy by the

162
00:07:47,280 --> 00:07:49,680
same amount for the trip back, so both

163
00:07:49,760 --> 00:07:52,160
rates rise by the same factor.

164
00:07:52,240 --> 00:07:54,240
They were equal at the old mixture, so

165
00:07:54,280 --> 00:07:56,640
they are still equal
there, and the vessels

166
00:07:56,760 --> 00:07:58,600
settle at the same mixture.

167
00:07:59,040 --> 00:08:01,680
If you said B ends with more ammonia,

168
00:08:01,920 --> 00:08:04,960
remember a catalyst
changes how soon, not how

169
00:08:05,120 --> 00:08:05,840
much.

170
00:08:06,160 --> 00:08:07,920
If you said the vessels hold the same

171
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:11,280
early on, remember B's rates are larger.

172
00:08:12,500 --> 00:08:12,900
Two.

173
00:08:13,140 --> 00:08:15,940
Iron raises the forward
rate and the reverse

174
00:08:16,060 --> 00:08:17,540
rate by the same factor.

175
00:08:18,020 --> 00:08:20,580
They were equal, so they stay equal, and

176
00:08:20,660 --> 00:08:23,060
the amount of ammonia does not change.

177
00:08:23,380 --> 00:08:25,940
If you said it shifts
toward ammonia, check

178
00:08:26,060 --> 00:08:27,860
whether one rate pulled ahead.

179
00:08:28,180 --> 00:08:30,740
If you said only the forward rate rises,

180
00:08:30,940 --> 00:08:33,380
remember the iron lowers
the activation energy for

181
00:08:33,460 --> 00:08:35,620
the trip back by the same amount.

182
00:08:36,834 --> 00:08:39,473
So when a question adds a catalyst, ask

183
00:08:39,594 --> 00:08:41,953
whether it changes
the speed or the mixture.

184
00:08:42,434 --> 00:08:45,073
It changes the speed, in both directions.

185
00:08:45,634 --> 00:08:48,033
On a graph, draw the same final level

186
00:08:48,114 --> 00:08:50,673
reached sooner, or no change at all if

187
00:08:50,714 --> 00:08:52,673
the mixture was already at equilibrium.

188
00:08:53,234 --> 00:08:56,193
For any other change,
ask which rates change

189
00:08:56,354 --> 00:08:57,953
and whether by the same factor.

190
00:08:58,674 --> 00:09:01,553
Next: putting a number
on where an equilibrium

191
00:09:01,634 --> 00:09:02,353
sits.

192
00:09:03,567 --> 00:09:05,006
Here are the key notes.

193
